this is a really great question because  everyone wants to know how we might you  know improve regenerative outcomes and  human patience right and so one way that  you can really start to think about how  to do that is to ask the question what  are the genes that enable axolotls to  regenerate so well and if you have the  whole genome then you can consider all  the genes because you know you have the  identity of all the genes and so what we  can do is we can say what are the genes  that help X levels are necessary for X  levels to regenerate and then we can  compare those genes to the genes in the  human and we can say okay do humans have  these genes or do they not have these  sheets and for once the humans might not  have we can say what would happen if you  gave for instance a mammal that gene for  instance what would be the consequences  could you at some level improve  regeneration maybe even just a little  bit and then another thing you can do is  say okay what about for the genes that  both X levels and humans in mice all of  them have are they a hundred percent  identical you know do they make the  exact same protein or not and do all the  same cells make that kind of protein etc  so you can go in and actually compare  you know point by point to see exactly  how similar are different they are and  that will give you some clues about you  know where they might where they might  be different and if they're important  for regeneration you could say well  maybe the human one is perhaps broken in  this spot right so that would be really  important it's also really important  experimentally for us to be able to  assign function to particular genes the  gold standard in genetics is to remove  them so to delete the gene and then see  what the consequences on the thing  you're studying so in this case  regeneration and with the technologies  that we have now so for instance CRISPR  editing technologies you can do that if  you know what gene you're studying but  if y